Title: The Conjurer's Revenge
Credit: Adapted from the tale by Charles W. Chesnutt (1899)
Order: 4
Production Code: 1x04

FADE IN:

EXT. MARS ARCHIE McMILLAN'S STORE - DAY

AMB: a country-store porch; flies; a mule stamping somewhere

Julius, in his Sunday clothes, sits on a barrel at the end of the porch and takes a long look at a man holding a horse by the bridle: a club-footed man, who walks with a curious twist.

[[Uncle Julius McAdoo]] turns his head from the club-footed man to the lens.

JULIUS (TO CAMERA)
Dat man yander, w'at is holdin' de hoss. Look at his foot. Look good.

The club-footed man shifts the reins to his other hand. His foot turns inward at the ankle.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
Ax 'im how he come by dat foot, en he'll call you a ole fool, en he ain' spoke ter you sence.

MUSIC: BED IN - slow, a plucked banjo and a mule's bray in the distance, almost comical, almost not

JULIUS (CONT'D)
I doan ax you ter b'lieve it. I des ax you ter set a minute.

SUPER: "THE CONJURE WOMAN"

EXT. THE VINEYARD - FRONT PIAZZA - AFTERNOON

An early-spring Sunday: the air a perfect balance of warm and cool. John and Annie sit in rocking-chairs; John with a novel he is throwing down in disgust; Annie, with a missionary report she is ploughing through conscientiously.

[[John]] tosses the novel aside. Julius is coming across the yard under the elms, in his Sunday clothes, dignified.

JOHN
Have a seat, Julius.

JULIUS
No, thanky, boss. I'll des set here on de top step.

ANNIE
Oh, no, Uncle Julius. Take this chair. You will find it much more comfortable.

Julius grins, and sits in the chair, awkwardly.

JOHN
Julius, I'm thinking of setting out scuppernong vines on that sand-hill where the three persimmon-trees are, and planting watermelons between the vines to pay for my first year's work. The new railroad will be finished by midsummer, and I can ship the melons North and get a good price.

JULIUS
Ef you er gwine ter hab any mo' ploughin' ter do, I 'spec' you'll ha' ter buy ernudder creetur, 'ca'se hit's much ez dem hosses kin do ter 'ten' ter de wuk dey got now.

JOHN
I had thought of that. I think I'll get a mule. A mule does more work and doesn't require as much attention as a horse.

JULIUS
I would n' 'vise you ter buy no mule.

JOHN
Why not?

JULIUS
Well, you may 'low hit's all foolis'ness, but ef I wuz in yo' place, I would n' buy no mule.

JOHN
That isn't a reason. What objection have you to a mule?

Julius considers for a very long moment.

JULIUS
Fac' is, I doan lack ter dribe a mule. I 's alluz afeared I mought be imposin' on some human creetur. Eve'y time I cuts a mule wid a hick'ry, 'pears ter me mos' lackly I's cuttin' some er my own relations, er somebody e'se w'at can't he'p deyse'ves.

ANNIE
(severely)
What put such an absurd idea into your head?

JULIUS
I dunno ez hit's wuf w'ile ter tell you dis. I doan ha'dly 'spec' fer you ter b'lieve it. Does you 'member dat club-footed man w'at hilt de hoss fer you de yuther day w'en you was gittin' out'n de rockaway down ter Mars Archie McMillan's sto'?

JOHN
Yes, I believe I do remember seeing a club-footed man there.

JULIUS
Did you eber see a club-footed nigger befo' er sence?

JOHN
(thinking)
No. I can't remember that I ever saw a club-footed colored man.

JULIUS
You en Mis' Annie would n' wanter b'lieve me, ef I wuz ter 'low dat dat man was oncet a mule?

JOHN
No, I don't think it very likely that you could make us believe it.

ANNIE
Why, Uncle Julius! What ridiculous nonsense!

Julius settles back, wounded. It takes John some diplomacy to coax him on. A long, dull afternoon is ahead of them.

JOHN
Come, Julius. We'd like to hear it.

Julius, with a sigh, begins.

JULIUS
W'en I wuz a young man, dat club-footed nigger--his name is Primus--use' ter b'long ter ole Mars Jim McGee ober on de Lumbe'ton plank-road...

MUSIC: BED OUT

SUPER: "BEFORE THE WAR."

EXT. McGEE PLANTATION - NIGHT (BEFORE THE WAR)

A cabin row in moonlight, shutters closed. A man steps out of a door with a banjo under his arm and the practiced lightness of someone who has done it before. [[Primus]], quick, broad-smiling, in a clean shirt.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Dis yer Primus wuz de livelies' han' on de place, alluz a-dancin', en drinkin', en runnin' roun', en singin', en pickin' de banjo; 'cep'n' once in a w'ile, w'en he 'd 'low he wa'n't treated right 'bout sump'n ernudder, he 'd git so sulky en stubborn dat de w'ite folks could n' ha'dly do nuffin wid 'im.

At the big house, a candle goes out. A white man on the porch, pretending he did not see.

JULIUS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
De w'ite folks purten' lack dey did n' know it, fer Primus was dange'ous w'en he got in dem stubborn spells, en dey 'd ruther not fool wid 'im.

EXT. THE FREE SETTLEMENT - NIGHT

A dance in a yard. A fiddle, a banjo, a jug going round the outside of the circle. Primus, in the middle, dancing.

SFX: fiddle, banjo, stamping, laughter

He sings. The girls laugh. It is two o'clock in the morning, and he is very happy.

EXT. COTTON FIELDS - NIGHT

Primus, walking home by the nigh-cut, whistling, banjo on his shoulder, staying clear of the road where the patrollers ride.

SFX: far off, hooves on the main road

At a fence corner, a grunt. He stops. A second grunt. He goes over. On a pile of pine straw, a fine fat shote.

Primus looks at the shote. The shote looks back. Primus turns and starts for home.

SFX: low drone, like a distant hornet

He takes one step forward with one foot. The other foot takes two steps back. He tries again. His whole body turns toward the shote against his will. His face goes from mild to puzzled to a little afraid.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Fus' thing you know Primus foun' hisse'f 'way up de road wid de shote on his back.

Primus on the road, shote over his shoulders, walking, looking down at himself as if watching a man he doesn't know.

JULIUS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Ef Primus had 'a' knowed whose shote dat wuz, he 'd 'a' manage' ter git pas' it somehow. Ez it happen', de shote b'long ter a cunjuh man w'at libbed down in de free-nigger sett'ement.

MUSIC: STING - low

EXT. THE FREE SETTLEMENT - DAWN

A cabin among the pines. A man in the doorway: older, broad, heavy-lidded, a long coat, unmistakably from somewhere else. [[The Guinea Conjurer]] looks down the empty road toward the McGee plantation. His mouth is a line. He goes inside and lights a lamp.

INT. THE CONJURER'S CABIN - DAWN

Shelves of gourds and bottles. A big iron pot. He crushes something in a mortar, slowly, with a stone, while the lamp burns.

SFX: grinding stone, a hornet drone, rising

EXT. McGEE PLANTATION - MORNING

The horn blows. In the cabin row, men file out toward the field. A white overseer walks down the row and looks into Primus's cabin. The bed is empty. A banjo on the nail.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Primus did n' go ter wuk w'en de hawn blow, en w'en de oberseah wen' ter look fer him, dey wa' no trace er Primus ter be 'skivered nowhar.

EXT. THE SWAMP EDGE - DAY

Dogs, following a scent, along the edge of a swamp. They stop. They circle. They lose it.

JULIUS (V.O.)
His marster a'vertise' him in de papers, en offered a big reward. De nigger-ketchers fotch out dey dogs, en track' 'im down ter de aidge er de swamp, en den de scent gun out; en dat was de las' anybody seed er Primus fer a long, long time.

INT. McGEE PLANTATION - CABIN - NIGHT

A young woman sits by a cold hearth: [[Sally]], young and light-skinned, good-looking, alone. She is waiting. She waits a month, then another month. She stops waiting.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Primus lef' a wife behin' 'im,--a monst'us good-lookin' yaller gal, name' Sally. W'en Primus had be'n gone a mont' er so, Sally 'mence' fer ter git lonesome, en tuk up wid ernudder young man name' Dan.

EXT. PATESVILLE - OLD WAGON-YARD - DAY

A country street. A bar-room with a sign: SANDY CAMPBELL. [[Jim McGee]], tall, leathery, stands in front of it.

A poor white man comes down the street and approaches him, casual.

POOR WHITE MAN
Mars Jim, you wanter buy a mule?

MARS JIM
I dunno. It 'pen's on de mule, en on de price. Whar is de mule?

POOR WHITE MAN
Des 'roun' back er ole Tom McAllister's sto'.

Behind the store, tied to a post: a very fine mule. When the mule sees Mars Jim, he whinnies, as to someone he knows.

Mars Jim walks around him. The mule looks at him with eyes that are a little too knowing.

MARS JIM
(frowning)
Dey 'pears ter be sump'n fermilyus 'bout dis mule's face. 'Spesh'ly his eyes.

POOR WHITE MAN
My brer raise' 'im on Rockfish Creek.

MARS JIM
Fifty dollars.

POOR WHITE MAN
(after a beat)
Done.

EXT. McGEE PLANTATION - TOBACCO PATCH - DAY

The mule, in the pasture, makes for the tobacco patch at the unfenced side and eats two whole rows.

JULIUS (V.O.)
De fus' thing de new mule done, w'en he was turnt inter de pastur', wuz ter make fer de terbacker-patch. De beastisses doan none un 'em eat terbacker. Dey doan know w'at 's good! Terbacker is lack religion, de good Lawd made it fer people.

A boy staked him out on a halter after that.

EXT. THE McADOO PLANTATION - WINEPRESS - DAY

A man named 'Dolphus has left the mule standing in the yard of a winepress while he goes for beef from [[Mars Dugal' McAdoo]]'s smokehouse. A tub of fresh-pressed grape juice. A half-full barrel of wine, boards over the top, a rock on the boards.

The mule walks by the tub. He puts his nose to the rock, shoves it off, lifts the boards. He drinks, gravely, as if from duty.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Dat mule drunk! He be'n drinkin' de wine!

He staggers, circles the yard, and falls over on his side like a dead thing. 'Dolphus comes out with the beef and finds him.

DOLPHUS
Fo' de Lawd! Dat mule's drunk!

A crowd gathers. They throw water on him. He does not wake.

JULIUS (V.O.)
De darkies all made a great 'miration 'bout de mule gittin' drunk. Dey never had n' seed nuffin lack it in dey bawn days.

EXT. McGEE PLANTATION - COTTON FIELD - DAY

The mule plows. At the end of a turn-row, [[Dan the Plowman]], who is holding the lines, stops, and looks around for the overseer. Sally is coming down the row with a basket.

DAN
Hoddy, honey. How you feelin' dis mawnin'?

SALLY
Fus' rate.

Neither of them looks at the mule. The mule has turned his head and is looking at Sally as hard as he can; his ears are up, his lips working, a soft whinny like a man's voice, low in his chest.

DAN
Yas, honey. En you gwine ter feel fus' rate long ez you sticks ter me. Fer I 's a better man dan dat low-down runaway nigger Primus dat you be'n wastin' yo' time wid.

He lets go of the plough-handle. He puts his arm around Sally.

SFX: a mule's snort, sudden

Something has him by the scruff of the neck and flings him over into the cotton. Sally flees. The mule stands in the furrow, calm as a Sunday morning.

Dan gets up, spitting dirt. He looks round for the overseer. There is none. He looks at the mule.

DAN (CONT'D)
(furious)
You!

He picks up a stick and lams the mule. The mule takes it; the mule, in fact, looks humble. At the turn he catches a plough-line round his hind leg. Dan reaches down, carelessly, to free it. The mule kicks him clean over the fence into a brier-patch.

INT. McGEE PLANTATION - CABIN - NIGHT

Dan, battered and scratched, on his pallet. The window is open. The mule's head appears in the window, lips drawn back off his teeth. He grins. He snaps.

Dan, up on one elbow, scream building in his throat.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Dan wuz layin' dere on his pallet, w'en he heard sump'n bangin' erway at de side er his cabin. W'at should he see but de noo mule's head stickin' in de winder, wid his lips drawed back over his toofs, grinnin' en snappin' at Dan des lack he wanter eat 'im up.

SFX: the door hammered by hooves

Next morning, in the door-frame, deep scored marks of hooves.

SALLY
(looking at the door)
I thought you had a nightmare.

DAN
(weakly)
I reckon I did.

EXT. THE FREE SETTLEMENT - DAY

A camp-meeting tent; a hundred people; hymns; a long bench at the front. On the mourners' bench, kneeling on the bare ground, [[The Guinea Conjurer]], hands clasped, head bowed, eyes wet.

JULIUS (V.O.)
'Long 'bout de middle er de summer dey wuz a big camp-meetin' broke out down on de Wim'l'ton Road, en nigh 'bout all de po' w'ite folks en free niggers in de settlement got 'ligion, en lo en behol'! 'mongs' 'em wuz de cunjuh man w'at own' de shote w'at cha'med Primus.

SFX: hymn, a hundred voices, a tambourine

MUSIC: SOURCE BED IN - hymn

JULIUS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Dis cunjuh man wuz a Guinea nigger, en befo' he wuz sot free had use' ter b'long ter a gent'eman down in Sampson County. De cunjuh man say his daddy wuz a king, er a guv'ner, 'way ober yander in Affiky whar de niggers come fum, befo' he was stoled erway en sol' ter de spekilaters.

INT. THE CONJURER'S CABIN - NIGHT

The conjurer in bed, grey, small, and cold. A cold from kneeling. Rheumatism has drawn him up. He sends a message by a boy.

JULIUS (V.O.)
He kep' gittin' wusser en wusser, 'tel one day he sont word up ter Mars Jim McGee's plantation, en ax' Pete, de nigger w'at tuk keer er de mules, fer ter come down dere dat night en fetch dat mule.

EXT. THE CONJURER'S CABIN - NIGHT

[[Pete]], on the bridled mule, at the door of the cabin. He ties the mule to the post and knocks.

PETE
(nervous)
Brer Conjurer?

CONJURER (O.S.)
Pull de string.

INT. THE CONJURER'S CABIN - NIGHT

Pete lifts the latch and goes in. The conjurer, pale and weak, on the bed, a single candle.

CONJURER
Is you fotch' de mule?

PETE
Yas.

CONJURER
Brer Pete. I's be'n a monst'us sinner man, en I's done a power er wickedness endyoin' er my days; but de good Lawd is wash' my sins erway, en I feels now dat I's boun' fer de kingdom. En I feels, too, dat I ain' gwine ter git up fum dis bed no mo' in dis worl'. I wants ter ondo some er de harm I done.

He points a shaking finger at a shelf.

CONJURER (CONT'D)
You 'member dat shote I was up ter yo' plantation inquirin' 'bout las' June?

PETE
Yas. I 'member yo' axin' 'bout a shote you had los'.

CONJURER
I done knowed yo' marster's Primus had tuk de shote, en I wuz boun' ter git eben wid 'im. So one night I cotch' 'im down by de swamp on his way ter a candy-pullin', en I th'owed a goopher mixtry on 'im, en turnt 'im ter a mule, en got a po' w'ite man ter sell de mule, en we 'vided de money. But I doan want ter die 'tel I turn Brer Primus back ag'in.

He gestures at gourds and bottles; Pete sets them on a stool. Then the mule is led in. He sees the bed, lowers his head, and starts toward it.

CONJURER (CONT'D)
Hol' on dere, Brer Primus! I's monst'us weak, en ef you 'mence on me, you won't nebber hab no chance fer ter git turn' back no mo'.

The mule stops. He understands.

The conjurer works his roots; the mule's ears become a man's ears; then the rest of the head, shoulders, arms. Primus, half-man, half-mule, shaking, straining, on all fours.

MUSIC: BED IN - low strings, slow

CONJURER (CONT'D)
(weakly, to Pete)
Gimme a drink er dem bitters out'n dat green bottle on de she'f yander. I's gwine fas', en it'll gimme strenk fer ter finish dis wuk.

Pete finds a bottle on the mantelpiece in the corner. It is too dark to tell its color. He holds it to the old man's lips. The old man takes a big mouthful and screams.

CONJURER (CONT'D)
You gimme de wrong bottle, Brer Pete! Dis yer bottle 's got pizen in it, en I's done fer dis time, sho'! Hol' me up, fer de Lawd's sake, 'tel I git th'oo turnin' Brer Primus back!

Pete props him up. The old man, grey now, dying, goes on working the roots with fingers that no longer obey him. Primus's body becomes a man's, all but one foot.

The old man gets the foot half turned and drops the roots.

CONJURER (CONT'D)
I can't do no mo' fer you, Brer Primus. But I hopes you will fergib me fer w'at harm I done. I knows de good Lawd done fergib me, en I hope ter meet you bofe in glory. I sees de good angels waitin' fer me up yander, wid a long w'ite robe en a starry crown, en I'm on my way ter jine 'em.

His head falls back. The candle gutters. Primus, kneeling, looks down at his own foot, which is turned in at the ankle, half hoof, half man.

MUSIC: STING - a single slow note

EXT. McGEE PLANTATION - DAY

Primus, limping, comes back up the lane, with Pete beside him. The quarter stands very still and watches them come.

JULIUS (V.O.)
De darkies all made a great 'miration w'en Primus come back. Mars Jim let on lack he did n' b'lieve de tale de two niggers tol'.

MARS JIM
Primus runned away, en stay' 'tel he got ti'ed er de swamps, en den come back on me ter be fed. En dat foot -- he mus' hab got his foot smash', er snake-bit, er sump'n.

He does not tie Primus up. He does not mention the mule. He looks at the road a little too long.

JULIUS (V.O.)
De niggers all notice' dey marster did n' tie Primus up, ner take on much 'ca'se de mule wuz gone. So dey 'lowed dey marster must 'a' had his s'picions 'bout dat cunjuh man.

EXT. THE VINEYARD - FRONT PIAZZA - DUSK

Julius finishing; John settling back; Annie, with her missionary report in her lap, unmoved.

ANNIE
That story does not appeal to me, Uncle Julius, and is not up to your usual mark. It isn't pathetic, it has no moral that I can discover, and I can't see why you should tell it. In fact, it seems to me like nonsense.

Julius looks puzzled and pained.

JULIUS
I's sorry, ma'm, ef you doan lack dat tale. I can't make out w'at you means by some er dem wo'ds you uses, but I's tellin' nuffin but de truf. Co'se I did n' see de cunjuh man tu'n 'im back, fer I wuz n' dere. But I be'n hearin' de tale fer twenty-five yeahs, en I ain' got no 'casion ter 'spute it.

He rises, and straightens his Sunday coat.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
Dey 's so many things a body knows is lies, dat dey ain' no use gwine roun' findin' fault wid tales dat mought des ez well be so ez not. F' instance, dey's a young nigger gwine ter school in town, en he come out heah de yuther day en 'lowed dat de sun stood still en de yeath turnt roun' eve'y day on a kinder axletree. I tol' dat young nigger ef he didn' take hisse'f 'way wid dem lies, I 'd take a buggy-trace ter 'im; fer I sees de yeath stan'in' still all de time, en I sees de sun gwine roun' it, en ef a man can't b'lieve w'at 'e sees, I can't see no use in libbin'.

JOHN
(a little amazed)
Julius.

JULIUS
En ernudder thing w'at proves de tale 'bout dis ole Primus is de way he goes on ef anybody ax' him how he come by dat club-foot. I axed 'im one day, mighty perlite en civil, en he call' me a' ole fool, en got so mad he ain' spoke ter me sence. Hit's monst'us quare. But dis is a quare worl', anyway yer kin fix it.

He puts on his hat, and, at the step, turns back.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
Ef you makes up yo' min' not ter buy dat mule, suh, I knows a man w'at 's got a good hoss he wants ter sell. I'm gwine ter pra'rmeetin' ter-night, en I'm gwine right by de man's house, en ef you 'd lack ter look at de hoss, I'll ax 'im ter fetch him roun'.

JOHN
(after a pause)
Oh, yes. You can ask him to stop in, if he is passing. There will be no harm in looking at the horse, though I rather think I shall buy a mule.

Julius nods, tips his hat, and walks off across the lawn.

EXT. THE VINEYARD - MORNING

SUPER: "THE NEXT MORNING."

A man leads a fine-looking horse up the lane. John, in his shirtsleeves, goes round it twice, runs a hand along its flank, looks at its teeth. The owner asks a large price, then a smaller price; John pays.

JOHN
(to himself, satisfied)
A very fine animal.

INT. THE VINEYARD - STABLE - DAY

SUPER: "A MONTH LATER."

John, in the stable doorway, stares. The horse stands with one eye clouded, the other not much better. It coughs. Its hocks are swollen. It is broken-winded and going lame.

SFX: a horse's long wheezing cough

JOHN
(slowly)
It's blind in one eye, and the sight of the other is defective.

INT. THE VINEYARD - STABLE - NIGHT

SUPER: "TWO MONTHS LATER."

The horse, down on the straw, in the grip of a colic. John, in his nightshirt, with a lantern. The horse lets out a long sigh and dies. John looks at the lamp, and at the dead horse, for a long time.

EXT. THE VINEYARD - STABLE YARD - DAY

A mule, in the traces, with a rockaway. Julius, in the box, with a long whip. He does not touch the mule with it. He drives gently, a little tenderly, as if the mule might be a relation.

JULIUS (V.O.)
(over, soft)
Eve'y time I cuts a mule wid a hick'ry, 'pears ter me mos' lackly I's cuttin' some er my own relations.

INT. THE VINEYARD - PARLOR - DAY

John at a window, watching the rockaway go by. Annie, sewing.

JOHN
Annie, I've had reason to suspect that Julius may have played a more than unconscious part in that horse transaction. There is the man who sold it to me, and there is the fact that the following Sunday Julius appeared in a new suit of store clothes I had seen displayed in Mr. Solomon Cohen's window.

ANNIE
(without looking up)
He may have saved the money.

JOHN
I haven't paid him any money recently, and he has no property to mortgage. I'll take his advice in small doses from now on.

Annie keeps sewing.

ANNIE
(a faint smile)
Perhaps it was only a kind thought.

JOHN
Perhaps.

EXT. MARS ARCHIE McMILLAN'S STORE - DAY

Julius in his new suit, on the porch, with the club-footed man, who is holding a horse. They are speaking low. Julius says something. The club-footed man laughs, short and hard, and turns his back on him.

EXT. THE VINEYARD - DUSK

Julius on the log, in his store suit, still too new.

JULIUS (TO CAMERA)
Mis' Annie say it wa'n't pa'thetic. I's sorry. Dey's mo' sorrer in a mule dan she knows.

He brushes a bit of lint from his sleeve.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
But I's tellin' you, ef I wuz gwine ter buy a hoss, I'd tek a mule fus'. En ef I wuz gwine ter buy a mule, I'd look him in de eye.

He rubs a thumb over the lapel.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
Nex' week I's gwine ter tell you 'bout a little pickaninny, en a rabbit foot. Dat one's gwine ter be 'bout Mis' Annie, whuther she knows it er no.

MUSIC: STING - a single banjo, then a mule's bray, then silence

FADE OUT.

THE END
